  abstract = {Definitions:                 "I propose the following definition of the nation: (5) "it [the nation] is an imagined political community - and imagined as both inherently limited and sovereign." (6) - Anderson only gives a factual description!                    a) imagined: all communities exceeding the face-to-face community of the village are imagined, what distinguishes them is the "style in which they are imagined". Imagined does not mean fabricated or consciously constructed (as Gellner allegedly believes) (6)                    b) limited: thus nation exists only in the plural (7). The potential stretch of these communities was inherently limited" (46) by the factual "fatality of human linguistic diversity." (43)                    c) sovereign: "because the concept was born in an age in which Enlightenment and Revolution were destroying the legitimacy of the divinely-ordained, hierarchical  dynastic realm." (7) - extremely weak argument                    d) community: "the nation is always conceived as a deep, horizontal comradeship. Ultimately it is this fraternity that makes it possible, over the past two centuries, for so many millions of people, not so much to kill, as willingly to die fur such limited imaginings.", i.e. it rests on a basic assumption of the fraternity of equals (7) which could give the basic legitimization for the claim of sovereignty, though Anderson does not rise this point.                    - from a) could follow that there is no nation-in-itself without a nation-for-itself, for it is bound to being conceived as a nation, compare Haas on that issue.                 Development in at least two stages:                 a) a formative phase of largely arbitrary conjunction of the basic conditions (45) the blueprint was provided by the Americas (81) and a "French Revolution" as created by the print media (Hobsbawm) and made into a model (80)                 b) then this could be and was taken as a "formal model to become imitated" (45). "the 'nation' proved to be an invention on which it was impossible to secure a patent. It became available for pirating by widely different, and sometimes unexpected hands." (67)                    - by mid-19th century "[…] imposed certain 'standards' from which too-marked deviations were impermissible." (81)                 Cultural Roots                 a) decline of religiously imagined communities                    - Nationalism gains momentum when the old systems of belief lost their appeal but suffering and contingency had still to be explained: "It is the magic of nationalism to turn chance into destiny." (12)                 Strong affinity with religious symbols because "The century of the Enlightenment, of rationalist secularism brought with it its own modern darkness. With the ebbing of religious belief, the suffering which belief in part composed did not disappear." (11)                    - With their sacred languages and their claim of universality the allowed anybody for admission (12-6)                    - reasons for decline:                         [I) discovery of the non-European world (16-8)]                         I) decline of the sacred languages and rise of vernaculars (Bloch: "Latin was not only the language in which teaching was done, it was the only language taught.") (18)                         II) decline of legitimacy of dynastic rule as divinely given (19-). These dynasties that expanded through war fare and intermarriage (20) were not just transnational but actually non-national at all and are usually foreigners to the country they are ruling (Gellner) (21) - implicitly: The decline can be seen in the rise of imperialism; whereas a king has divine legitimization the emperor occupies power (i.e. Roman Empire)                        III) decline of a "temporality in which cosmology and history were indistinguishable" (36)                 b) "invention" of  linear chronological time (23)                 - opposed to the old simultaneity of past, present, and future a new "homogenous, empty time" (Benjamin) gains precedence (24)                 - "The idea of a sociological organism moving calendrically through homogenous, empty time is a precise analogue of the idea of the nation, which also is conceived as a solid community moving steadily down (or up) history." (26)                 - gives four examples of literary works (26-32)                 - newspapers:                    - this homogenous time is exemplarily produced by newspapers: the only bond that connects the events reported in a newspaper is the date that is printed on the frontpage (33)                    - newspapers are a mass-produced commodity that is almost simultaneously consumed, which creates a mass-ceremony and the imagined community of the readers, who are "well aware that the ceremony he performs is being replicated simultaneously by thousands (or millions) of others whose existence he is confident, yet of whose identity he has not the slightest notion." (35)                 Spread of National Consciousness                 "What, in a positive sense, made the new communities imaginable was a half-fortuitious, but explosive, interaction between a system of production and productive relations (capitalism), a technology of communication (print), and the fatality of human linguistic diversity." (42-3) "[…] it would be a mistake to equate this fatality with that common element in nationalist ideologies which stresses the primordial fatality of particular languages and their association with particular territorial units." (43)                 a) rise of the vernacular languages, due to "age of mechanical reproduction" (37): "Then and now the bulk of mankind is monoglot. The logic of capitalism thus meant that once the elite Latin market was saturated, the potentially huge markets represented by the monoglot masses would beckon." (38) "These fellow-readers, to whom they were connected through print, formed, in their secularism particular, visible invisibility, the embryo of the nationally imagined community." (44) this was facilitated by                    I) drive towards Ciceroan Latin due to the humanists, no relation with the ecclestial life anymore (39)                    II) the Reformation, creating new reading publics among merchants and women (39-40)                    III) trend towards centralization of states (40), that created state-languages (41)                 b) "print-capitalism gave a new fixity to language, which in the long run helped to built the image of antiquity so central to the subjective idea of the nation." (44) "Certain dialects inevitably were 'closer' to each print-language and dominated their final forms." (45) dito for vernaculars that "already used that print-language and appeared correspondingly menacing to those who did not." (78)                 Creole Pioneers                 The first national liberation movements in the two Americas were not based on linguistic arguments (47-50), why so?                 - all the South American republics had been administrative units from 16th cent onward (52)                 - They became seen as a fatherland by journeys of the creole elites as "meaning-creating experience" (53) These journeys were limited to the colonial periphery's centre (56-60) and provided people with the experience a limited and clearly defined territory and of having counterparts from all over the country (55). In order to communicate within their strata and with the centre they had to employ a standardized language-of-state (56)                 - The other important role played the journalist-cum-printer-cum-publisher of provincial newspapers                 Old Languages, New Models                 - On the assumption that every people has its own language (Herder) (67-8), dictionaries (often monolingual) and encyclopaedias were compiled and philology became the first science that established the principle of evolution (Hobsbawm) (70-1), which led the way to cultural (e.g. modern Greek nationalism as instigated by philhellenism) (72). This caused the proliferation of formalized languages and nation-states based in these (74).                 - The highly educated agents of early linguistic nationalism depended on a market and consumers: their families, wives and children (75). The economic bourgeoisie was formed as a class of replicas: "Here a class which, figuratively speaking, came into being as class only in so many replications. […] they did come to visualize in a general way the existence of thousands and thousands like themselves through print-language." (77)                 - "[…] typical, however, was a coalition of lesser gentries, academics, professionals, and businessmen, in which the first often provided leaders of 'standing', the second and third myths, poetry, newspapers, and ideological formulations, and the last money and marketing facilities." (79)                 After the initial stage  "'The new middle-class intelligentsia of nationalism had to invite the masses into history; and the invitation card had to be written in in a language they understand.'" (Nairn) (80)                 Official Nationalism and Imperialism                 - The top-down approach of nation-building (e.g. Russification) "developed after and in reaction to , the popular national movements proliferating in Europe since the 1820s." (86)                 - "official nationlism" inside Europe: "These nationalism were historically 'impossible' until after the appearance of popular linguistic-nationalisms, for, at bottom, they were responses by power-groups - primarily, but not exclusively, dynastic and aristocratic - threatened with exclusion from, or marginalization in, popular imagined communities." (110)                 The Last Wave (post WW II)                 - areas congruent with colonial boundaries (114)                 - the journeys are not longer exclusive to administrative elites but the masses are travelling within that area (115)                 - the spread of modern-style education proliferated the common language and imperial 'official nationalism' (116) (Gellner). The new "colonial" school were uniform and installed a spatial hierarchy (121). Thus, at a certain time a particular group of adolescents actually experienced being a nation. (124)                 - difference to earlier nationalisms: they consciusly admitted their being newcomers: foundation of various "young" movements (e.g. SE Asia) (119)                 Patriotism and Racism (141-54)                 The Angel of History (155-62)                 Census, Map, Museum (163-85)                 Memory and Forgetting (187-                 - Rénan: und allen ist etwas gemeinsam, dass sie gemeinsam etwas vergessen haben - oder so ähnlich},
